    overview:
      "Welcome to electoral systems and law in the United States. This lecture is about the rules of the game, not how to play it well within them — that is Campaign Strategy's job. Here we ask a different question: why does the American system produce exactly two viable parties almost everywhere, why does a candidate sometimes win the presidency while losing the national popular vote, and who actually gets to decide where a district's lines are drawn. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a working politician or election lawyer actually encounters them. We start with the single mechanical fact that explains more about American politics than any other, plurality voting in single-member districts, before turning to its stranger cousin, the Electoral College, which applies the same winner-take-all logic one level up with a very different apportionment base. We then turn to the machinery that decides which candidates even appear on a general-election ballot: redistricting, primaries, and ballot-access law. From there we turn to money and identity, the two most litigated fronts in American election law, before closing with the decentralized administrative system that runs it all and the live reform debates trying to change pieces of it. Keep one question running underneath everything: does this rule aggregate votes the way it appears to on paper, or does it quietly convert a certain kind of vote into more power than an identical vote cast somewhere else.",
