        id: "electoral-us-fptp-house",
        title: "First-past-the-post: plurality voting in single-member districts",
        explanation:
          "Start with the single rule everything else sits on top of. Elections to the House of Representatives, and to the vast majority of state and local offices, use first-past-the-post: each district elects exactly one member, and whichever candidate wins the most votes wins the seat outright, with no requirement to clear 50%. If three candidates split a district 36-34-30, the candidate with 36% takes the entire seat, and the other 64% of voters in that district get no representation from their preferred choice at all. This is mechanically simple and that simplicity is the whole point: voters can see immediately who won and who lost, with no formula, no remainder, and no coalition math standing between the ballot and the result. The French political scientist Maurice Duverger observed that this exact mechanical property, a plurality rule with no runoff, tends over time to compress a political system down toward two dominant parties, a pattern now generally called Duverger's Law [1]. The mechanism is straightforward: a vote for a third-place candidate in a winner-take-all race is, in the race's own terms, a wasted vote, so rational voters and donors drift toward whichever of the two frontrunners more closely matches their preference, starving third candidates of exactly the support they would need to become genuinely competitive.",
