          "Now turn to the system's most distinctive and most frequently misunderstood feature. The president is not elected by a national popular vote; each state is allocated a number of electors equal to its total congressional delegation, its House seats plus its two senators, with the District of Columbia separately guaranteed three electors under the Twenty-Third Amendment, for 538 electors total and 270 needed to win. In 48 states and DC, the state's entire slate of electors goes to whichever candidate wins that state's popular vote, a winner-take-all rule adopted by state legislatures rather than required by the Constitution itself; Maine and Nebraska instead award most electors by statewide winner and the rest by whichever candidate wins each individual congressional district. Because electors are allocated using the same plus-two Senate bonus in every state regardless of population, a vote cast in a small state carries meaningfully more weight in the Electoral College's math than a vote cast in a large one, and because 48 states apply winner-take-all on top of that, a candidate can assemble exactly the right combination of competitive states to reach 270 while losing the nationwide popular vote outright, which has happened five times in the country's history, most recently in 2000 and 2016.",
