    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one underlying pattern: a system built almost entirely on plurality, winner-take-all mechanics, applied at every level from the individual House district up through the presidency itself, and defended or challenged through a decentralized, heavily litigated legal structure rather than a single national rulebook. First-past-the-post and the Electoral College are the two core mechanical rules, one for the legislature and one for the presidency, and both produce the same wasted-vote logic that Duverger's Law explains and that sustains the country's remarkably stable two-party system. Redistricting, primaries, and ballot-access law are the three gates that decide who even reaches the general-election ballot, each shaped by the same underlying incentive to protect whoever currently holds power within the existing two-party structure. Campaign-finance law and voter-identification law are the two most actively litigated fronts specifically because the Supreme Court's own rulings, Citizens United and Shelby County chief among them, reshaped the available legal space dramatically within the last two decades, and decentralized administration is the operational reality every one of these rules actually has to run through on the ground, county by county, with no national authority to smooth out the differences. The live reform debates closing this lecture are best understood as targeted attempts to change one specific mechanical rule at a time, since the system's overall structure makes any larger, constitutional-amendment-level change essentially impossible to achieve. As you follow any American election story from here forward, ask which of these ten mechanical rules is actually driving the behavior you are reading about, since a remarkable amount of what looks like simple political dysfunction turns out, on closer inspection, to be entirely rational behavior given the specific rule actually in play.",
