    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one organizing choice: majoritarianism over proportionality, applied consistently across the legislature and the presidency alike, specifically to manufacture governable majorities rather than the fragmented coalitions of the pre-1958 Fourth Republic. The two-round legislative system and the directly elected presidency are the two core mechanical designs, both sharing the same absolute-majority-then-runoff logic while differing in exactly who qualifies for the second round, and the parrainage requirement is the gate deciding who even reaches the presidential first round in the first place. Désistement républicain is the alliance behavior the two-round, multi-candidate-runoff structure itself produces, the clearest proof that France's own brand of majoritarianism sustains genuine multipartism in a way a single-round plurality system never could, a nuance traceable directly back to Duverger's own study of French electoral history. The CNCCFP and the Conseil constitutionnel are the two institutions policing the system's integrity, one over campaign money and one over the results themselves, while cohabitation and the indirectly elected Senate are the two structural features that reveal just how much of the Fifth Republic's apparent presidential strength actually depends on political alignment with the separately elected legislature rather than on the presidency's own constitutional text alone. The live debate over introducing proportional representation closing this lecture is the same proportionality-versus-stability tension running through every electoral system this course covers, here playing out as an unresolved argument within one country's own politics rather than as a comparison across different countries' different historical choices. As you follow French electoral politics from here forward, ask whether a given institution or dispute is really about majoritarian stability, proportional fairness, or the alliance logic the two-round structure itself generates between rounds, since nearly everything distinctive about how France runs its elections traces back to that one founding, deliberate choice.",
