    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one organizing mechanism, applied across genuinely uneven geography: D'Hondt proportional representation within closed party lists, calculated separately in 52 provinces whose own size, not the formula itself, ultimately decides how proportional any given province's result actually turns out to be. The formula and the closed-list design are the starting mechanics, while provincial magnitude and the deliberately guaranteed rural minimum-seat floor are the geography that gives those mechanics their real texture, making the formally uniform 3% threshold largely irrelevant everywhere except the handful of largest provinces where it might actually bind. Regionally concentrated parties converting a modest national vote share into substantial, pivotal seat totals is the direct strategic consequence of that same province-by-province, non-pooled design, a structural asset no dispersed national party can replicate without the system itself changing. LOREG is the single statute underlying every one of these mechanics, administered day to day by the Junta Electoral Central hierarchy and, in financing matters, policed retrospectively by the Tribunal de Cuentas, with the expedited amparo electoral judicial track standing as the system's final, time-sensitive safeguard when administrative review alone is not enough. The Senate's contrasting majoritarian design is a reminder that Spain's own electoral-system choices were never applied uniformly even within the country's own constitution, and the live reform debates closing this lecture, provincial disproportionality and closed-list candidate control, are simply the same proportionality-versus-territorial-representation and party-power-versus-voter-choice tradeoffs this course's fundamentals describe everywhere, here still working themselves out province by province, election after election.",
